# __PLUGIN_NAME__

A plugin for the [Auth0 CLI](https://github.com/auth0/auth0-cli), written in Go with the [go-auth0](https://github.com/auth0/go-auth0) SDK. It runs as `auth0 __PLUGIN_NAME__` and calls the Management API through the CLI, which never gives the plugin a real token.

The sample command lists the clients of your tenant. Make it your own.

## Requirements

- Go 1.26 or later
- The Auth0 CLI, with plugin development turned on: `export AUTH0_CLI_PLUGIN_DEV_MODE=true`

## Layout

`main.go` starts the command, `commands/` holds one file per command and the registry that finds it, and `plugin/` holds the shared code: environment and SDK client, output, error text with exit codes, and pagination. `plugin.json` is the manifest: commands, flags and the Management API scopes each command may use.

The Go module is named `example.com/__PLUGIN_NAME__`, a path reserved for examples, so change it in `go.mod`, the imports and the `-X` flag of `.goreleaser.yaml` when you publish the project.

## Build and run

```sh
go mod tidy          # also writes go.sum; commit it
go build -o __PLUGIN_NAME__ .
```

Link the build to the CLI once. The link reads `plugin.json` and the executable again on every run, so after a change you only rebuild.

```sh
auth0 plugin dev link
auth0 plugin approve __PLUGIN_NAME__    # a person reviews the scopes; repeat when they change
auth0 __PLUGIN_NAME__
auth0 __PLUGIN_NAME__ --json
```

`make build`, `make test` and `make lint` do the same from the Makefile. `plugin.json` is checked with `auth0 plugin dev validate`.

## Scopes

The CLI proxy refuses every call that needs a scope the command did not declare, and the plugin then says which scope is missing. Declare scopes under `permissions.scopes` of the command in `plugin.json`, then approve again. The sample command needs `read:clients`.

