## Add a command

1. Copy `commands/clients.go` to `commands/<name>.go`. Change the name, the help text and the code in the run function, and keep the call to `Register` in `init`.
2. Add the command to `commands` in `plugin.json`, with its description and the scopes it needs.
3. Rebuild. A command that is not the root command runs as `auth0 __PLUGIN_NAME__ <name>`.

Flags: declare them on the command's `flag.FlagSet` and list them under `flags` in `plugin.json`; the flags of the CLI itself (`--json`, `--tenant` and so on) are handled for you. Use `out.JSON` to choose between `out.PrintJSON` and `out.PrintTable`, and `plugin.Collect` for every page of a list call. Results go to stdout, anything else to stderr.

## Environment

The CLI sets these for every run. Only `plugin/client.go` and `plugin/output.go` read them.

| Variable | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `AUTH0_PLUGIN_DOMAIN` | Address of the CLI proxy (`host:port`) |
| `AUTH0_PLUGIN_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN` | A secret for this run that lets the plugin use the proxy |
| `AUTH0_PLUGIN_CA_CERT` | PEM file with the certificate of the proxy |
| `AUTH0_PLUGIN_JSON`, `AUTH0_PLUGIN_JSON_COMPACT` | `true` when the user asked for JSON output (agent mode turns it on) |
| `AUTH0_PLUGIN_TENANT` | The tenant the run is for |

Exit codes: 0 on success, 1 when the command fails, 2 when the arguments are wrong.

## Releasing

Edit nothing by hand: run one command, then merge the pull request it opens.

```sh
make release BUMP=patch             # or minor, or major
make release VERSION=1.2.3          # an exact version
make release BUMP=patch DRY_RUN=1   # show the steps, change nothing
```

It needs `git`, `gh` (`gh auth login`) and a clean `main` that matches `origin`. It creates `release/v<version>`, raises `version` in `plugin.json`, commits `chore: release v<version>` with your own git settings, pushes and opens the pull request. A keyword refuses a prerelease version; give the exact version then.

Merging runs the Release workflow, which:

1. runs the tests (`ci.yml`) on the merged commit,
2. tags it `v<version>`,
3. runs GoReleaser: seven platforms, each packed with `plugin.json` into `<name>_<version>_<platform>.tar.gz` (`.zip` on Windows), plus `checksums.txt`,
4. publishes them as a GitHub release with generated notes.

If a run fails half way, re-run it or run the workflow by hand on `main`: it finishes what is missing and does nothing once the release is complete. The first push of a new project releases the scaffolded version. The workflow listens to `main`; change that in `release.yml` for another branch name.

| File | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `scripts/release/` | The program behind `make release` |
| `.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Vet, test, build and lint; the release calls it too |
| `.github/workflows/release.yml` | The steps above |
| `.github/actions/read-version/` | Reads the version and tells whether its tag and release exist (`.github/scripts/`) |
| `.goreleaser.yaml` | Builds, archives and checksums; `make release-snapshot` runs it into `dist/` without publishing |

The workflow stops at the GitHub release. Signing, attestations and publishing to the plugin catalog come later.

